1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,720
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,610
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,400
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,300
Today, our topic is the window.

8
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:22,580
Sitting by a window, for one minute.

9
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:26,310
Ben, what is outside your window right now?

10
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:28,180
Hi Mia.

11
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:28,820
Hi everyone.

12
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:35,420
Right now, a roof, a grey sky, and one tree, Mia.

13
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:37,000
Half of a tree.

14
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:39,720
The other half is behind a wall.

15
00:00:39,830 --> 00:00:42,700
And I look at it every hour, for one minute.

16
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:44,180
What about you?

17
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:45,840
What is outside yours?

18
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:50,100
A tree, a roof, and a cat on the roof, Ben.

19
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:52,640
But I only know that since last month.

20
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:55,200
Because last month, I moved a chair.

21
00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:58,040
That is the whole story, almost.

22
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:03,000
My chair was in the corner of my room, facing the wall, next to the desk.

23
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:05,120
For four years.

24
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:07,960
Four years of paint.

25
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:09,960
Four years facing a wall, Mia?

26
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:14,320
I did not know your room had a window at all.

27
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:14,730
And then?

28
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:20,640
And then one Sunday, for no reason, I pulled it to the window, Ben.

29
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:22,030
And I sat down.

30
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:23,800
And I looked outside.

31
00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:27,000
And I sat there for twenty minutes.

32
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:28,260
Twenty.

33
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:30,020
I did not read.

34
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:31,880
I did not look at my phone.

35
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:33,620
I looked at a tree.

36
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:35,080
Just a tree.

37
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:38,420
And there was a tree behind me the whole time.

38
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:40,460
Four years.

39
00:01:41,230 --> 00:01:46,039
"There was a tree behind you the whole time." Mia, that is our topic today.

40
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:47,960
So here is our plan.

41
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,330
In part one, we talk about our own windows and walls.

42
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:57,840
In part two, we ask why one minute at a window does something to the body.

43
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:01,360
And I have a true story for that, from a hospital.

44
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:06,520
In part three, we share simple ways to use a window every day.

45
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:08,180
Does that sound good?

46
00:02:09,009 --> 00:02:10,039
That sounds good, Ben.

47
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:14,280
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

48
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:16,640
You will hear it again.

49
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:19,800
And if there is a window near you, turn a little toward it.

50
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:22,120
That is the whole homework.

51
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:23,480
Okay, Ben.

52
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:24,359
Part one.

53
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:27,840
You asked me why I faced the wall for four years.

54
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:29,300
I did, Mia.

55
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:33,540
Four years is a long time to look at paint.

56
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,300
Did you choose the wall?

57
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:36,740
I never chose the wall, Ben.

58
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:39,960
I chose the desk, and the wall came with it.

59
00:02:40,829 --> 00:02:42,519
The desk was where the work was.

60
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,240
And I think that is how most of us live.

61
00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:47,780
We do not choose to face the wall.

62
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:52,940
We choose the desk, the screen, the sofa, and the wall comes with it.

63
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:55,820
The wall comes with the desk, Mia.

64
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:58,840
And let me ask about those twenty minutes.

65
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:01,160
What did you actually look at?

66
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:03,520
A tree, a roof.

67
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:05,880
But what did the eyes do?

68
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:08,000
They wandered, Ben.

69
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:09,360
That is the word.

70
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:12,040
At the desk, my eyes are pinned.

71
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:15,140
One point, thirty centimeters away.

72
00:03:16,020 --> 00:03:18,120
At the window, they wandered.

73
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:24,540
Tree, then roof, then sky, then the cat, then back to the tree.

74
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:28,040
Nothing held them, and nothing asked.

75
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:32,820
And after twenty minutes, something behind my eyes stopped pushing.

76
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:38,170
"Something behind the eyes stopped pushing." Mia, I know that feeling.

77
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:40,789
And I have a window, and I earned it.

78
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:44,320
At my old job, the desks by the window were for the managers.

79
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:47,660
I sat inside, by a wall, for three years.

80
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:51,320
Then I changed jobs, and my new desk was by a window.

81
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:53,620
Managers get the windows, Ben.

82
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:55,080
That says something.

83
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:58,890
Why do the people with the most power take the window?

84
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,240
Because they know, Mia.

85
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:02,370
Or their bodies know.

86
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:13,300
Nobody says, "I want the window because my eyes need a far thing." They say, "I want the window because I am the manager." But the reason underneath is the same.

87
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:15,160
The eyes want out.

88
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:16,980
Everybody's eyes.

89
00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:19,940
We just give the window to the person who can take it.

90
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:22,220
A window desk, Ben.

91
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:24,080
And did it change something?

92
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:27,620
Or is a desk a desk, wall or window?

93
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,140
It changed my afternoons, Mia.

94
00:04:30,970 --> 00:04:33,670
At the wall desk, at three o'clock, I was finished.

95
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:34,750
Tired.

96
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:36,480
Heavy.

97
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:41,570
At the window desk, at three o'clock, I looked out for one minute, and the tiredness went somewhere.

98
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:43,100
Not all of it.

99
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:46,700
But some of it went out of the window, and I did not go with it.

100
00:04:47,770 --> 00:04:52,060
"The tiredness went out of the window." Ben, where did it go?

101
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:53,800
What is the window doing?

102
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:56,160
Two things, I think, Mia.

103
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:58,230
First, distance.

104
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:03,780
At a desk, the eyes look at something thirty centimeters away, all day.

105
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:04,740
A screen.

106
00:05:04,860 --> 00:05:05,280
A page.

107
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:09,920
The eye muscles hold that distance, hour after hour, tight.

108
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:12,620
And the window is far.

109
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:14,400
The eyes let go.

110
00:05:14,460 --> 00:05:16,840
They rest on something far.

111
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:19,740
The eyes were tight, and now they are not, Ben.

112
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:21,640
I felt that at the window.

113
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:23,680
Something let go.

114
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:25,220
And the second thing?

115
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:26,879
Movement, Mia.

116
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:29,750
The window moves, and the wall does not.

117
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:31,620
A cloud goes past.

118
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:33,580
A bird lands.

119
00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:36,039
The light changes from white to gold.

120
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:37,420
Nothing important.

121
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:39,000
But the eyes like it.

122
00:05:39,009 --> 00:05:40,620
They follow it, for one minute.

123
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:46,000
And following something slow and unimportant is rest for the eyes and for the head.

124
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:49,020
The eyes like slow things, Ben.

125
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:50,980
But the phone moves too.

126
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:52,160
Fast.

127
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:53,740
Is that not the same?

128
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:56,960
The phone moves, Mia, but it asks.

129
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:00,080
Every movement on the phone asks for something.

130
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:01,200
Look here.

131
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:02,220
Tap this.

132
00:06:02,820 --> 00:06:03,360
Read that.

133
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:05,560
The cloud asks for nothing.

134
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:07,020
It goes past.

135
00:06:07,660 --> 00:06:08,980
You look, or you do not.

136
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:10,680
That is the difference.

137
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:13,220
Movement that asks is work.

138
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:16,160
Movement that asks nothing is rest.

139
00:06:16,930 --> 00:06:18,650
The window only has the second kind.

140
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:27,360
"The tree is the only thing in the room that asks for nothing." Ben, that is why I stayed twenty minutes.

141
00:06:28,020 --> 00:06:29,440
Nothing was asking.

142
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:31,440
The desk asks.

143
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:33,220
The phone asks.

144
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:35,780
The tree does not ask.

145
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:38,900
Okay, so that was part one.

146
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:40,219
Our windows.

147
00:06:40,750 --> 00:06:42,080
Now, part two.

148
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:46,300
Why does one minute at a window do something to the body?

149
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:49,170
Ben, your story is from a hospital.

150
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:50,780
It is, Mia.

151
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:52,220
Okay, part two.

152
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:54,260
This is a true story.

153
00:06:55,040 --> 00:07:00,640
In 1984, a scientist named Roger Ulrich published a small study in the journal Science.

154
00:07:01,360 --> 00:07:03,740
He looked at patients in a hospital in Pennsylvania.

155
00:07:04,340 --> 00:07:07,500
Forty-six people, all after the same operation.

156
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:09,140
Gallbladder surgery.

157
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:10,680
Same doctors.

158
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:11,740
Same nurses.

159
00:07:12,130 --> 00:07:13,180
Same rooms.

160
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:14,720
Same food, even.

161
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:15,980
Same everything, Ben?

162
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:20,470
Same doctors, same rooms, same operation?

163
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:22,260
Then what was different in the rooms?

164
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:23,720
The window, Mia.

165
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:26,650
Half of the rooms looked at a group of trees.

166
00:07:27,130 --> 00:07:29,600
The other half looked at a brick wall.

167
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:30,710
That was the only difference.

168
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:32,840
Trees, or a brick wall.

169
00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:36,590
And Ulrich looked at the records for nine years of patients.

170
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:39,400
Nine years of records, Ben.

171
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:42,200
So it was not one week of patients.

172
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:43,960
It was many.

173
00:07:43,990 --> 00:07:46,330
And the doctors did not know about the trees?

174
00:07:46,940 --> 00:07:49,480
Nobody was thinking about the trees, Mia.

175
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:51,620
The rooms were given by chance.

176
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:53,660
Whoever came in got the next empty bed.

177
00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:57,280
Some got trees, some got the wall.

178
00:07:57,340 --> 00:07:59,120
That is why the study is good.

179
00:07:59,160 --> 00:07:59,980
Nobody chose.

180
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:02,380
And the trees still made a difference.

181
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:05,160
Trees or a brick wall, Ben.

182
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:06,140
And?

183
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:08,840
What did nine years of records say?

184
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:12,060
The patients with the trees went home sooner, Mia.

185
00:08:12,630 --> 00:08:14,380
About a day sooner.

186
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:16,540
That is one less night in a hospital bed.

187
00:08:17,310 --> 00:08:20,490
They asked for less pain medicine, and weaker pain medicine.

188
00:08:21,130 --> 00:08:23,460
And the nurses wrote fewer bad notes about them.

189
00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:28,820
"Patient is upset." "Patient needs encouragement." Fewer of those.

190
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:31,540
Only from what the eyes saw from the bed.

191
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:33,600
A day sooner, Ben?

192
00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:34,960
From a window?

193
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:37,520
Only from what the eyes saw?

194
00:08:38,300 --> 00:08:40,580
That is a big thing from a small thing.

195
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:42,380
Only from that, Mia.

196
00:08:43,010 --> 00:08:44,120
The body was the same.

197
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:45,840
The medicine was the same.

198
00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:47,800
The trees were the difference.

199
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:50,420
Trees do not give medicine.

200
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:52,090
They give the eyes a place to go.

201
00:08:52,950 --> 00:08:55,540
And I think the study means something simple.

202
00:08:55,570 --> 00:08:58,080
The eyes are not only for reading.

203
00:08:58,120 --> 00:09:02,360
The eyes are a door, and what comes through the door goes into the body.

204
00:09:03,340 --> 00:09:11,300
"The eyes are a door." Ben, my body was not sick, but it was tired, and the wall was not helping.

205
00:09:11,989 --> 00:09:15,620
I was feeding my eyes a wall for four years.

206
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:18,370
And the patients with the wall needed more medicine.

207
00:09:19,220 --> 00:09:20,820
Same thing, smaller.

208
00:09:21,700 --> 00:09:23,180
Same thing, smaller, Mia.

209
00:09:23,770 --> 00:09:26,440
And I think we stopped believing in the outside.

210
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:28,140
We live inside now.

211
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:33,440
Inside the house, inside the office, inside the car, inside the phone.

212
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:38,100
The outside is a place we cross to get to another inside.

213
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:40,900
And the window is the outside, delivered.

214
00:09:40,940 --> 00:09:41,850
No shoes needed.

215
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:46,960
"The outside, delivered, no shoes." Ben, I like that.

216
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:49,800
Because I am lazy about the outside, honestly.

217
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:54,120
The walk from our walking episode is good, and I do it, but not every day.

218
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:56,400
The window is every day.

219
00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:57,900
One minute a day.

220
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:00,320
It is the outside for tired people.

221
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:02,600
The outside for tired people, Mia.

222
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:08,800
And for busy people, and for people who cannot leave the bed, like Ulrich's patients.

223
00:10:08,810 --> 00:10:10,580
They could not walk to the trees.

224
00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:12,660
The trees came to the window.

225
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:14,240
That is the whole gift of a window.

226
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:16,640
The trees came to the window, Ben.

227
00:10:17,520 --> 00:10:21,420
So a window is a small trip outside, without leaving.

228
00:10:21,480 --> 00:10:23,780
Is that why it works, even for a minute?

229
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:25,300
I think so, Mia.

230
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:27,300
The body knows it is inside.

231
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:28,960
But the eyes do not.

232
00:10:29,020 --> 00:10:31,320
The eyes only know what they see.

233
00:10:31,340 --> 00:10:33,640
Show them a tree, and they think they are in a park.

234
00:10:34,420 --> 00:10:39,160
For one minute, the eyes are in the park, and the rest of you comes along.

235
00:10:39,240 --> 00:10:40,980
That is the trick of the window.

236
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:43,500
It lies to the eyes, in a good way.

237
00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:45,240
Can I ask about the minute, Ben?

238
00:10:45,740 --> 00:10:48,020
Because you keep saying one minute.

239
00:10:48,050 --> 00:10:49,360
And I want to be honest.

240
00:10:49,640 --> 00:10:51,640
A minute at a window is long.

241
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:53,620
It is long, Mia.

242
00:10:54,260 --> 00:10:57,700
The first time, I looked at my watch after twenty seconds.

243
00:10:57,710 --> 00:11:00,060
The eyes wanted to go back to the screen.

244
00:11:00,100 --> 00:11:01,380
They were pulling.

245
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:04,360
Because the eyes are used to being fed every second.

246
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:05,960
The window feeds slowly.

247
00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:08,480
So the first minutes are a small fight.

248
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:11,720
Twenty seconds and you looked at your watch, Ben.

249
00:11:12,280 --> 00:11:14,200
That is the eyes begging for the screen.

250
00:11:14,820 --> 00:11:16,600
What did you do at second twenty-one?

251
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:19,140
I looked at the tree again, Mia.

252
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:20,260
That is all.

253
00:11:20,380 --> 00:11:23,690
I told my eyes, "Not yet." Like a child at a shop.

254
00:11:24,340 --> 00:11:26,390
And at second forty, they stopped pulling.

255
00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:30,239
And at the end of the minute, I did not want to go back.

256
00:11:30,249 --> 00:11:33,160
The eyes only fight for the first half of the first minute.

257
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:36,000
After that, they remember what they are for.

258
00:11:36,490 --> 00:11:43,420
"The first minutes are a small fight, and then the minute is not enough." Ben, that was exactly my week.

259
00:11:43,880 --> 00:11:47,580
Day one, twenty minutes felt like a gift, because I was so tired.

260
00:11:48,080 --> 00:11:50,460
Day two, five minutes felt like forever.

261
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:53,520
Day five, one minute was not enough again.

262
00:11:53,940 --> 00:11:54,830
The eyes learned.

263
00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:57,840
The eyes learned, Mia.

264
00:11:57,900 --> 00:11:59,140
That is the whole thing.

265
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:01,760
Okay, so that was part two.

266
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:02,830
Why the window works.

267
00:12:03,140 --> 00:12:05,780
We talked about Ulrich and the hospital with the trees.

268
00:12:06,120 --> 00:12:07,780
We said the eyes are a door.

269
00:12:08,180 --> 00:12:10,800
And we said the first minutes are a small fight.

270
00:12:11,260 --> 00:12:12,680
Now, part three.

271
00:12:12,740 --> 00:12:15,060
Simple ways to use a window every day.

272
00:12:15,540 --> 00:12:16,760
Mia, you go first.

273
00:12:17,560 --> 00:12:19,040
Okay, part three.

274
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:21,200
My first idea is the morning window, Ben.

275
00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:24,560
Before the phone, from our small steps episode.

276
00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:26,680
I sit at the window with my tea.

277
00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:27,900
Three minutes.

278
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:30,280
I look at the tree and the roof.

279
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:33,320
And the day is wider after that.

280
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:34,070
Not longer.

281
00:12:34,789 --> 00:12:35,200
Wider.

282
00:12:35,710 --> 00:12:40,680
"The day is wider after that." Mia, my first idea is the hourly minute.

283
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:43,220
At my window desk, I made a small rule.

284
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:45,780
Every hour, one minute at the window.

285
00:12:46,150 --> 00:12:48,350
Stand up, look far, sit down.

286
00:12:48,920 --> 00:12:53,330
And I noticed that one minute an hour is worth more than the coffee at three o'clock.

287
00:12:54,780 --> 00:12:56,300
Worth more than the coffee, Ben.

288
00:12:56,660 --> 00:12:57,880
That is a big claim.

289
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,119
My second idea is for English.

290
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:02,880
Because there is a window in language learning, too.

291
00:13:03,560 --> 00:13:04,060
Listening.

292
00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:09,360
When I listen to Spanish for a long time, my head gets tight, like my eyes at the desk.

293
00:13:09,960 --> 00:13:11,600
So now I listen at the window.

294
00:13:12,180 --> 00:13:13,380
Spanish at the window, Mia?

295
00:13:13,950 --> 00:13:15,370
Does the window help the words?

296
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:16,580
Or only the eyes?

297
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:17,960
It does, Ben.

298
00:13:18,450 --> 00:13:21,360
Spanish at the window is easier than Spanish at the desk.

299
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:24,200
The tight head is a bad head for language.

300
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:26,440
Words need room to land.

301
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,420
And the eyes on a far tree give the head room.

302
00:13:29,980 --> 00:13:31,840
So listen to this podcast at a window.

303
00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:32,880
Or a door.

304
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:34,760
Let the words land.

305
00:13:35,230 --> 00:13:40,800
"Let the words land." Mia, my second idea is for the people who have no window.

306
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:42,280
Because some are listening.

307
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:43,760
A basement office.

308
00:13:44,220 --> 00:13:45,780
A room with a wall.

309
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:50,100
And I want to say what Ulrich's patients could not do, and we can.

310
00:13:50,140 --> 00:13:50,840
We can move.

311
00:13:51,480 --> 00:13:52,940
Borrow a window.

312
00:13:52,950 --> 00:13:53,890
The kitchen at work.

313
00:13:54,420 --> 00:13:55,720
The corridor with a window.

314
00:13:56,360 --> 00:13:57,090
Sixty seconds.

315
00:13:57,680 --> 00:13:58,940
Borrow a window, Ben.

316
00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:02,460
And if there is no window at all, there is the door.

317
00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:05,120
A door to the street is a big window.

318
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:08,039
Sixty seconds outside the door.

319
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:10,000
The far thing is the point.

320
00:14:10,060 --> 00:14:11,500
Not the glass.

321
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:13,110
A gap between buildings counts.

322
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:15,660
Even a window in a door counts.

323
00:14:15,670 --> 00:14:24,800
"The far thing is the point." Mia, and one thing about the tree, because Ulrich's study was about trees, and I think trees are special, even in a window.

324
00:14:25,230 --> 00:14:27,300
A tree moves without going anywhere.

325
00:14:27,820 --> 00:14:29,960
Leaves, branches, light through them.

326
00:14:30,600 --> 00:14:33,120
It is the slowest alive thing you can look at.

327
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:38,100
"The slowest alive thing." Ben, my window tree is not beautiful.

328
00:14:38,620 --> 00:14:42,160
It is a city tree, half dead on one side, next to a bin.

329
00:14:42,760 --> 00:14:43,560
And it is enough.

330
00:14:44,240 --> 00:14:48,480
I watch it move in the wind for one minute, and something in me moves with it.

331
00:14:49,080 --> 00:14:50,610
A tree is the right speed.

332
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:53,920
A tree is the right speed, Mia.

333
00:14:54,500 --> 00:14:55,980
Can I tell you about my worst month?

334
00:14:56,400 --> 00:14:59,600
Because it shows what happens when we close the window without deciding to.

335
00:15:00,040 --> 00:15:00,780
Please, Ben.

336
00:15:01,340 --> 00:15:03,200
How do you close a window without deciding?

337
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:04,260
Curtains?

338
00:15:04,660 --> 00:15:05,120
A screen?

339
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:06,680
Curtains, Mia.

340
00:15:07,260 --> 00:15:09,040
Last winter, I closed the curtains.

341
00:15:09,440 --> 00:15:15,480
It was cold, and dark, and I thought, "Nothing to see." And I left them closed for six weeks.

342
00:15:15,520 --> 00:15:17,520
And my afternoons went back to the wall desk.

343
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:18,490
Tired.

344
00:15:18,980 --> 00:15:19,290
Heavy.

345
00:15:20,040 --> 00:15:21,160
I did not connect it.

346
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:26,900
Then one morning, I opened them to let the cat out, and there was a bird on the bare tree.

347
00:15:26,980 --> 00:15:29,380
A bird on the bare tree, Ben.

348
00:15:29,420 --> 00:15:31,650
And you had closed the door and called it winter.

349
00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:32,880
I had, Mia.

350
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:35,920
So my third idea is very small.

351
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:37,700
Leave the curtains open.

352
00:15:37,780 --> 00:15:38,500
Even in the dark.

353
00:15:39,220 --> 00:15:40,740
Even when there is nothing to see.

354
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:44,680
A bare tree in winter is still a tree, for the eyes.

355
00:15:45,460 --> 00:15:47,280
Ulrich's trees were not beautiful either.

356
00:15:47,880 --> 00:15:48,840
They were just trees.

357
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:51,080
The window is always on.

358
00:15:51,700 --> 00:15:52,800
Do not turn it off.

359
00:15:53,380 --> 00:15:55,220
The window is always on, Ben.

360
00:15:55,779 --> 00:15:56,499
Even at night?

361
00:15:57,100 --> 00:15:58,900
There is nothing to look at at night.

362
00:15:59,260 --> 00:16:00,840
There is the dark, Mia.

363
00:16:00,870 --> 00:16:01,960
And the dark is far.

364
00:16:02,550 --> 00:16:05,940
The eyes rest on the dark the way they rest on a tree.

365
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:07,160
A street light.

366
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:09,260
One window across the road.

367
00:16:09,270 --> 00:16:10,800
The moon, if you are lucky.

368
00:16:11,460 --> 00:16:14,800
Look at the far dark for one minute, and the day gets smaller.

369
00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:16,720
Night is a window too.

370
00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:22,340
"Night is a window too." Ben, my third idea is the evening window.

371
00:16:23,100 --> 00:16:27,660
After dinner, when the light goes, I sit at the window for the last five minutes of light.

372
00:16:28,270 --> 00:16:29,500
The sky does its thing.

373
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:32,260
Gold, then grey, then dark.

374
00:16:32,820 --> 00:16:35,040
And the day finishes in front of me.

375
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:35,880
Not stopped.

376
00:16:36,500 --> 00:16:37,040
Finished.

377
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:40,040
Every night, the same five minutes.

378
00:16:40,440 --> 00:16:47,440
"The day finished in front of you, not stopped." Mia, and my fourth idea is looking with someone.

379
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:50,020
Because the window is also a place for two people.

380
00:16:50,670 --> 00:16:53,310
My daughter and I sit at the window on Sunday mornings.

381
00:16:53,940 --> 00:16:54,520
Ten minutes.

382
00:16:55,160 --> 00:16:57,640
We look outside and say what we see.

383
00:16:57,650 --> 00:17:00,980
"A bird." "A man with a dog." "The cat again."

384
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:03,840
The window is the only screen we share, Ben.

385
00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:07,200
I do that with my mother, on the phone.

386
00:17:07,280 --> 00:17:13,780
I sit at my window, she sits at hers, four hundred kilometers away, and we tell each other what is outside.

387
00:17:14,340 --> 00:17:14,940
Her tree.

388
00:17:15,380 --> 00:17:16,020
My tree.

389
00:17:16,760 --> 00:17:18,680
Two windows, one call.

390
00:17:19,100 --> 00:17:20,540
Two windows, one call.

391
00:17:21,210 --> 00:17:23,340
Mia, that is beautiful.

392
00:17:23,380 --> 00:17:28,090
And one last idea, about the word "look." Not "watch." Look.

393
00:17:28,760 --> 00:17:30,140
Watching is for a screen.

394
00:17:30,680 --> 00:17:31,540
It has a story.

395
00:17:32,140 --> 00:17:33,440
Looking has no story.

396
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:35,000
You look at a roof.

397
00:17:35,540 --> 00:17:36,860
Nothing happens.

398
00:17:36,920 --> 00:17:38,300
The roof never speeds up.

399
00:17:39,040 --> 00:17:39,850
That is the rest.

400
00:17:40,640 --> 00:17:46,200
"The roof never speeds up." Ben, I want to put that on the wall, next to the window.

401
00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:50,160
And something strange happened at my window, in week two.

402
00:17:50,910 --> 00:17:52,600
I started to know the outside.

403
00:17:53,440 --> 00:17:54,520
The cat comes at eight.

404
00:17:55,060 --> 00:17:56,840
The man with the bag at eight fifteen.

405
00:17:57,560 --> 00:17:59,440
The light hits the roof at nine.

406
00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:02,659
The outside has a schedule, and I know it now.

407
00:18:03,620 --> 00:18:08,140
A small world, always there, behind the chair, Mia.

408
00:18:08,920 --> 00:18:10,400
That is the tree behind you again.

409
00:18:11,090 --> 00:18:12,900
And that is our message today.

410
00:18:12,940 --> 00:18:14,700
We face walls without choosing to.

411
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:19,040
The desk, the screen, the curtains.

412
00:18:19,060 --> 00:18:23,800
And the eyes were made for far things, slow things, alive things.

413
00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:25,020
A tree.

414
00:18:25,900 --> 00:18:26,360
A roof.

415
00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:27,720
A sky.

416
00:18:28,840 --> 00:18:29,360
One minute.

417
00:18:30,140 --> 00:18:31,120
The eyes are a door.

418
00:18:32,100 --> 00:18:32,520
Open it.

419
00:18:33,700 --> 00:18:35,040
"The eyes are a door.

420
00:18:35,640 --> 00:18:42,280
Open it." Ben, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.

421
00:18:43,380 --> 00:18:43,820
Window.

422
00:18:44,720 --> 00:18:45,640
Sit by a window.

423
00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:47,500
Borrow a window.

424
00:18:48,620 --> 00:18:48,940
Look.

425
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:51,900
To use the eyes, with no story.

426
00:18:52,900 --> 00:18:53,460
Look out.

427
00:18:54,120 --> 00:18:54,740
Look far.

428
00:18:55,920 --> 00:18:56,460
Minute.

429
00:18:57,380 --> 00:18:58,300
Sixty seconds.

430
00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:00,280
One minute at the window.

431
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:02,020
One minute an hour.

432
00:19:03,150 --> 00:19:03,540
Tree.

433
00:19:04,410 --> 00:19:06,020
The slowest alive thing.

434
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:08,620
A tree behind you.

435
00:19:08,680 --> 00:19:10,220
A tree is the right speed.

436
00:19:11,420 --> 00:19:12,480
Eye.

437
00:19:12,520 --> 00:19:13,440
The eyes wander.

438
00:19:14,080 --> 00:19:15,260
The eyes are a door.

439
00:19:16,420 --> 00:19:16,820
Window.

440
00:19:17,840 --> 00:19:18,080
Look.

441
00:19:19,180 --> 00:19:19,640
Minute.

442
00:19:20,920 --> 00:19:21,240
Tree.

443
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:22,780
Eye.

444
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:25,460
Five words, and one chair.

445
00:19:26,220 --> 00:19:27,240
And one big idea.

446
00:19:28,140 --> 00:19:28,980
Sit by a window.

447
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:32,480
Now, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?

448
00:19:33,380 --> 00:19:33,960
Yes, Ben.

449
00:19:34,340 --> 00:19:36,390
Everyone listening, here is my question.

450
00:19:37,260 --> 00:19:39,050
What is outside your window right now?

451
00:19:39,860 --> 00:19:40,930
Or the nearest window.

452
00:19:41,750 --> 00:19:44,150
Write it in the comments in one short sentence.

453
00:19:44,850 --> 00:19:54,000
"A roof and a grey sky." "A tree and a bin." And then, if you want, add, "I will look at it for one minute today."

454
00:19:54,100 --> 00:19:55,000
One minute today.

455
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:59,940
And if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.

456
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,520
We make a new episode every few days.

457
00:20:02,600 --> 00:20:06,800
Slow English, simple words, and one true story each time.

458
00:20:07,680 --> 00:20:10,010
The full text of this episode is in the description.

459
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:12,520
Read it at a window, if you can.

460
00:20:13,500 --> 00:20:15,250
Thank you for spending this time with us.

461
00:20:15,740 --> 00:20:18,290
Now, before you do anything else, look up.

462
00:20:19,040 --> 00:20:20,180
Find the far thing.

463
00:20:20,820 --> 00:20:24,100
A window, a door, a gap between buildings.

464
00:20:24,820 --> 00:20:25,380
One minute.

465
00:20:25,980 --> 00:20:26,980
Let the eyes go.

466
00:20:27,880 --> 00:20:29,160
There is a tree behind you.

467
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:30,540
There always was.

468
00:20:31,320 --> 00:20:32,040
Turn the chair.

469
00:20:32,480 --> 00:20:33,520
Look for one minute.

470
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:35,140
Look again tomorrow.

471
00:20:36,160 --> 00:20:36,740
This is Ben.

472
00:20:37,560 --> 00:20:38,380
And this is Mia.

473
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:40,500
This was Two Cups English.

474
00:20:41,220 --> 00:20:42,100
Sit by a window.

475
00:20:42,940 --> 00:20:43,950
We will see you next time.

